        "like": 4
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5acad9ecc9dba010c9c3ec68|full_name}: Are you saying VOIP was peaking at -15db in the mix or that y'all were driving the VOIP group +15db for balance? \n\nI obviously respect the decision to not change anything for now if it's gonna affect the experience. But ~long-term~ for feedback-sake, I think there might be a misunderstanding where the gain setting is being seen as a fader instead of an amplifier.\n\nRegular audio with +10db gain added is going to be as quiet or as loud as whatever the input / audio clip peaks at ...plus 10db. This default can be an issue because creators are going to have sources + clips that peak at varying levels (or some may even normalize peaks to 0db so they can do levels in Unity). So rhetorically, if they have an audio clip that's peaking at 0db with their volume set to 1; the added 10db gain means they're now clipping 10db. (っ °Д °;)っ Additionally, that 10db overdrive will definitely pass the master bus compression threshold depending on a player's volume settings. \n\nObviously feel free to take this with a grain of salt, but possibly a better option to try out would be to be cut rather than amplify. By that I mean, balance the game's overall mix out by only lowering volume even if it means everything is globally quieter. You'll have your balanced game mix with a bit of headroom - so on the master bus, you can simply add gain/amplify once to hit your desired loudness + use the newly-added compressor to tame any crazy transients. You'd have better staging + dynamics, and there shouldn't be a need to amplify anything on our end by default for balance!",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2019-06-29T22:40:02.043Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "The current iteration of `VRC_SpatialAudioSource` has the default gain level set to 10. Because this component ~currently~ seems to act as a conduit for ONSP Audio Source, this means you're driving the audio source 10db hotter than unity gain. \n\nThe default gain should be set to 0...and maybe even have the gain level option removed from the `VRC_SpatialAudioSource` to avoid confusion/redundancy. This would ensure that gain staging is managed by Unity's audio source component exclusively. Otherwise, what people hear in their editor may be louder at runtime.\n\nThis has been a long-standing thing, but it's more important to squash this now with the addition of limiting/compression on the master bus where 10db can cause audio to pass the compression threshold.",
